Postural neck stiffness and trapezius ache secondary to prolonged ergonomic strain
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Trapezius dull ache, Palpable taut muscle bands, Prolonged fixed posture history

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-033
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe neck pain and stiffness requiring evaluation to rule out cervical radiculopathy or meningism.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe neck stiffness and bilateral muscle ache. Urgent physical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate cervical range of motion, perform a cranial nerve examination, and definitively exclude life-threatening causes of neck rigidity such as meningitis or acute cervical disc prolapse.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My neck and shoulders are completely locked up and aching after sitting at my computer all day.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a bilateral dull aching pain in the upper trapezius and cervical paraspinal muscles, developing gradually over the past 12 hours. Reports a mild restriction in active cervical lateral flexion and axial rotation due to pain. Onset closely correlates with extended periods of fixed posture working at a poorly configured desk. Notes relief of the dull ache with targeted rest and gentle stretching. Complete absence of radiating radicular arm pain, numbness, or tingling. Denies fever or photophobia.
Functional Status: Fully independent, head movements are guarded.
Social/Safety Concerns: Poor home office ergonomics.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/80 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, holding head stiffly, no photophobic posturing or distress at rest.
Physical Exam: Neck examination reveals palpable taut bands within the trapezius muscle bellies bilaterally. Diagnostic triad confirmed: trapezius dull ache, palpable taut muscle bands, and prolonged fixed posture history. Normal sensory and motor examination of the bilateral upper extremities (5/5 strength). Intact symmetric deep tendon reflexes in the biceps and triceps. Absence of midline cervical spinous process tenderness. Negative Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs.
Environmental Observations: Laptop placed flat on a low dining table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter ibuprofen with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Postural neck stiffness and trapezius ache secondary to prolonged ergonomic strain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, negative meningeal signs, and lack of photophobia or severe global headache.

Cervical Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal upper extremity neurology, preserved reflexes, and lack of radiating pain down the arm.

Cervical Spine Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of physical trauma, no midline spinous tenderness, and gradual onset related to posture.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe cervical stiffness. Given that neck rigidity is a hallmark sign of potentially fatal intracranial infections or acute spinal cord compromise, delaying the physical assessment was unsafe. The urgent face-to-face evaluation allowed for a thorough neurological and musculoskeletal examination, confidently confirming a benign postural muscle spasm. This safe exclusion of meningism and radiculopathy prevented unnecessary emergency department neuroimaging and allowed for immediate conservative management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advice on alternating heat and cold therapy to the trapezius muscles. Recommendation for scheduled oral NSAIDs with food. Demonstration of gentle cervical range of motion exercises.
Positive Behavioral Change: Set a digital alarm across the room to go off every 45 minutes during desk work, physically forcing a break in the sustained ergonomic posture to silence the alarm, thereby preventing muscle ischemia.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Laptop stand and external keyboard for ergonomic correction.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks if the stiffness does not resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a sudden high fever, an excruciating headache, sensitivity to bright lights, or sudden weakness or numbness in your arms.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical musculoskeletal strain; underlying degenerative disc disease may contribute and requires routine evaluation if symptoms are chronic.
Clinical Course: The severe stiffness typically resolves over 48 to 72 hours with appropriate mobility and ergonomic correction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if pain begins shooting like an electric shock down your arm, or if you begin dropping objects from your hands.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Staring down at a laptop forces the muscles in the back of your neck to work continuously to hold your head up. They eventually run out of oxygen and go into a painful spasm. Heat relaxes them and restores blood flow.
Actionable Steps: Raise your screen to eye level. Do not push through the pain; take regular posture breaks. Use a heat pack for 15 minutes at a time.

SMS INFORMATION

